      "answer_text": "Use **`hermes-all-three`** (`hermes-plugins/hermes-all-three/`). It is one composed engine that applies all three practices.\n\n**What stops three separate plugins from running together:** Hermes allows only one context engine.\n\n- `PluginRegistration.register_context_engine` (`hermes_cli/plugins.py:717`) checks `if self._manager._context_engine is not None`. If an engine is already registered, it logs a warning and returns without registering the second one. This is documented in `hermes-plugins/CONTRACT.md:87-91`.\n- Selection works the same way: `context.engine: <name>` in `config.yaml` names a single engine, and the host uses that one instance as `agent.context_compressor`.\n- All three practices need the engine seams: `select_context`, `on_turn_complete` and `get_tool_schemas`. A second plugin would have no way to hook in.\n\n`hermes-all-three` gets around this by running the three practices inside one `select_context`. Its README and `engine.py:88` describe the order as D (context graph) projects, then B (tool disclosure) injects the catalog and folds, then A (relevance filter) rewrites oversized results. Each stage is fail-open, so an error in one stage doesn't block the others.\n\nThis limit is specific to Hermes. The Claude Code plugins I created don't have it, because hooks from several plugins can be active at once. `claude-code-all-three` is a convenience bundle there, not a requirement."
    },
